00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.760
Do microbes ever feel fear?

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.958
Or concern?

00:01:21.958 --> 00:01:23.378
Or trepidation?

00:01:23.378 --> 00:01:27.061
While they can’t of course tell us, they probably
don’t–

00:01:27.061 --> 00:01:30.049
at least not in ways that we could understand.

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:34.651
But we can tell that they definitely experience
stress.

00:01:34.651 --> 00:01:40.180
Now, that stress is not  terribly similar to
what we experience when tax season rolls around

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.180
or a traffic jam makes you late for an important
meeting.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.368
The microbial world is mercifully free of
such troubles.

00:01:47.368 --> 00:01:52.295
But at its core, what microbes experience
is stress nonetheless -

00:01:52.295 --> 00:01:58.127
the recognition of, and response to, less-than-ideal
circumstances.

00:01:58.127 --> 00:02:04.308
And when you think about it, of course the
microcosmos, can be a very stressful place -

00:02:04.308 --> 00:02:09.800
from shifts in food availability, to sudden
temperature swings, to changes in pH that

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.757
can begin picking apart a microbe molecule
by molecule.

00:02:13.757 --> 00:02:18.884
All in all, there’s a lot going on that
can get in the way of microbes

00:02:18.884 --> 00:02:20.580
living their best lives.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.786
So maybe it is no surprise that, naturally,
stressed microbes are everywhere.

00:02:27.952 --> 00:02:33.650
Like a bear going into hibernation through
a frigid winter, this tardigrade, also known

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:40.310
as a water bear, has seemingly given up trying
to face the stresses of the world.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:46.879
Instead, it is lying motionless in a state
of reduced metabolic activity, almost as if

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:51.540
it is simply waiting for all of its problems
to disappear.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:57.180
It has formed a durable and protective cyst,
and it will stay in there until more favorable

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.874
conditions arise.

00:02:58.874 --> 00:03:06.548
And it can stay encysted like this for months,
to be woken up by a change in seasons, perhaps.

00:03:06.548 --> 00:03:13.840
Microbes from all domains of life have evolved
similar abilities to outlast those trying times.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:20.390
And they do this by entering extended periods
of reduced metabolic activity, or dormancy.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:25.930
Because, while microbes can be found everywhere
you point your microscope, that doesn't mean

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.899
they can thrive just anywhere.

00:03:28.899 --> 00:03:33.810
Each species has its own preferences when
it comes to habitat, its own comfort zone

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.760
that is specific to its biology and way of
life.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:44.440
Holophyra ciliates, for example, prefer non-acidic
habitats that have plenty of dissolved oxygen.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:50.260
If these ciliates find themselves in a more
stressful acidic environment, they, too, form

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:55.519
cysts and sit tight, waiting for the world
to change.

00:03:55.519 --> 00:04:00.540
If they’re lucky, that change might arrive
in the form of heavy rains that increase the

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.251
pH of those puddles and pools that their cysts
lie dormant in.

00:04:05.251 --> 00:04:12.245
Sensing a more hospitable habitat, they re-awaken,
face the day, and go about their ciliate business.

00:04:14.378 --> 00:04:19.100
Now cysts are not the only way members of the
microcosmos can enter the liminal boundary

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.173
between life and death, though.

00:04:21.173 --> 00:04:24.650
These bacteria have found another way to cope.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:30.829
The brighter pockets at their tips are the
first stages of their last resort to stress.

00:04:30.829 --> 00:04:35.020
Maybe it’s a lack of nutrients in the sample,
maybe the pH levels don't suit them.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:40.569
Whatever it is that’s left them distressed,
their coping mechanism is unfolding.

00:04:40.569 --> 00:04:47.900
They’re each building a cellular bunker,
a spore, which we can see forming here.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.880
Their DNA and essential cellular machinery
will be almost completely dehydrated and tightly

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.405
packaged inside the spore’s thick envelope.

00:04:57.405 --> 00:05:02.919
Eventually, the spore will abandon the cell
altogether, jettisoning like an escape pod

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:06.728
as the mother cell undergoes a self-destruct
sequence.

00:05:06.728 --> 00:05:12.240
In their spore form, with their metabolism
at minimum possible levels, they can last

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:21.610
for decades or even centuries, protected from
the harshest of conditions, biding their time.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:27.130
But until then, they are part of the 
ecosystem’s microbial seed bank -

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:33.229
the repository of dormant microorganisms too
stressed out by local conditions to fully

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:39.480
live their lives, but also too stubborn to
die.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.319
Seed banks are a big deal in the microscosmos.

00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:47.949
They allow species to enter cold storage when
times are tough.

00:05:47.949 --> 00:05:52.924
In that seed bank, species have tapped out
of the stressful game of life or death.

00:05:52.924 --> 00:05:55.110
They’ve found a temporary third option.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:01.930
And when they awaken, whole microbial ecosystems
can simply pick back up where they left off.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:06.210
You can wake up a seedbank yourself and watch
it happen at home.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.960
All you need is some food and, of course,
a microscope.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.590
If you soak the vegetables and herbs destined
for your next meal in water overnight and

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:18.949
check the water under your microscope the
next day, you are pretty much guaranteed

00:06:18.949 --> 00:06:21.609
to spot some colpodids swimming around.

00:06:21.609 --> 00:06:24.753
You have just enticed them 
out of the seed bank –

00:06:24.753 --> 00:06:29.719
by providing them with a welcomingly wet habitat,
you triggered them to ‘excyst’.

00:06:29.719 --> 00:06:32.951
Not to exist, ‘ex-cyst’.

00:06:32.951 --> 00:06:37.910
Colpodid ciliates can be found in soils around
the world and they will often hitch a ride

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.894
on the surfaces of your food.

00:06:39.894 --> 00:06:45.000
Now, do not lose any sleep over the idea that
you are exposed to and are ingesting dormant

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.712
microbes throughout your day.

00:06:46.712 --> 00:06:50.729
That is just part and parcel of life on this
planet I promise, like you consume them just

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:56.640
breathing- it's just one of many of the bridges 
between us and the microcosmos.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.449
Encysted colpodids are not dangerous, they’re
just sleepy stowaways, making the trip from

00:07:02.449 --> 00:07:06.977
the soil, to the surface of a tomato, and
then, into your mouth.

00:07:11.442 --> 00:07:16.550
James, our master of microscopes, often tries
a few different tricks to coax stressed out

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.940
microbes back into excystence.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:24.505
And using different chemical cues, he can
activate the dormant cysts and spores

00:07:24.505 --> 00:07:26.289
of different organisms.

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:31.100
If he wants to awaken the seedbanks of microbes
that have a particular love of sulfur, for

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:35.350
example, he might add the yolk of a cooked
egg to the sample.

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:39.940
For others, the wake up call comes in the
form of fertilizer, which provides nitrogen

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:41.410
and phosphorus.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:46.379
This stage that we’re looking down at right
now can host an endless combination of casts

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:52.470
performing an endless number of plays, it
all comes down to direction.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:58.259
It seems wherever you look in the microcosmos,
microbes enduring stressful conditions can

00:07:58.259 --> 00:08:07.479
be found, suspended in a twilight zone between
life and death, each species sitting, waiting,

00:08:07.479 --> 00:08:12.507
holding out hope for their own version of 
paradise to appear.

